1
00:00:09,471 --> 00:00:12,790
All right,
let's jump into reliable transfer.

2
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,846
So to restate what the goal here is,

3
00:00:17,846 --> 00:00:22,609
is that we want to be able
to allow an application or

4
00:00:22,609 --> 00:00:30,528
a process that's running to be assured
that what they send is what is received.

5
00:00:30,528 --> 00:00:33,250
It's the same data, same order.

6
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:38,099
So if we're transferring
some information like, say,

7
00:00:38,099 --> 00:00:43,553
a file, contents, or
a message to direct them to do something,

8
00:00:43,553 --> 00:00:47,540
we want to make sure that that's received.

9
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:52,940
And so we talked about the two
main protocols, UDP and TCP.

10
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:57,930
So UDP does not offer this, but TCP does.

11
00:00:57,930 --> 00:01:02,596
And so here we're going to talk
about the mechanism TCP has to be

12
00:01:02,596 --> 00:01:06,280
able to ensure reliable transfer.

13
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:12,440
So first, let's understand why
we need this in the first place,

14
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:16,700
where packets can get lost or reordered.

15
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:20,142
So we've already seen all of this.

16
00:01:20,142 --> 00:01:27,200
So from the link layer, we saw
the concept of a frame or a packet error.

17
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:32,261
So we have this wire,
this transmission medium

18
00:01:32,261 --> 00:01:38,849
that one node is sending to the other,
but it's not perfect.

19
00:01:38,849 --> 00:01:43,300
And so bit flips and
other errors may occur.

20
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:49,770
And the Ethernet, for example,
has a field to be able to detect errors,

21
00:01:49,770 --> 00:01:55,320
but it doesn't have the ability
to correct those errors.

22
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:00,360
So what it does is it just drops
the frames that are in error.

23
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:06,315
A second place where we saw potential for

24
00:02:06,315 --> 00:02:11,376
unreliable transmission
occur was at the network

25
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:18,088
layer when we went into the router's
data plane architecture.

26
00:02:18,088 --> 00:02:23,901
So if you recall,
a router has all these line cards,

27
00:02:23,901 --> 00:02:31,053
and the picture shown here on
the right is the kind of processing.

28
00:02:31,053 --> 00:02:37,062
So the packet is received, it looks up the
IP address inside of the forwarding table,

29
00:02:37,062 --> 00:02:42,528
does some updates, and then it queues
the packets inside of a packet buffer.

30
00:02:42,528 --> 00:02:48,840
And this has a limited amount of
memory to be able to store packets.

31
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:53,348
And so if the incoming rate is
greater than the outgoing rate,

32
00:02:53,348 --> 00:02:59,340
then this buffer will fill up and
then we'll have to start dropping packets.

33
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:02,552
So how might this occur?

34
00:03:02,552 --> 00:03:07,120
How might we have an incoming rate
greater than an outgoing rate?

35
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:12,648
Well, if you recall,
we have a bunch of different inputs.

36
00:03:12,648 --> 00:03:18,774
Well, and the destination might all
be going out the same output port.

37
00:03:18,774 --> 00:03:22,495
So even if there's the same
speed on all of the ports,

38
00:03:22,495 --> 00:03:27,705
let's say it's ten gigabits per second,
well, we might be receiving ten

39
00:03:27,705 --> 00:03:33,232
gigabits per second on one input,
ten gigabits per second on another input.

40
00:03:33,232 --> 00:03:38,891
But then if all the traffic is going to
one output, they might only be able to,

41
00:03:38,891 --> 00:03:43,486
or they would only be able to
send at ten gigabits per second.

42
00:03:43,486 --> 00:03:47,909
And so that's how we're now
going to have this rate higher at

43
00:03:47,909 --> 00:03:51,800
the incoming level rather
than over the outgoing.

44
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:58,711
And the third kind of place where we saw

45
00:03:58,711 --> 00:04:04,616
a problem has to do
with packet reordering.

46
00:04:04,616 --> 00:04:09,637
So we saw in the network layer when
we talked about the control plane and

47
00:04:09,637 --> 00:04:11,520
decentralized routing.

48
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:18,060
Routing is meant to be dynamic,
to handle things

49
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:22,740
such as different changes
in configuration,

50
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:27,720
changes for policies, handling failures.

51
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:32,670
And so let's say we have an initial
state where there's a path that goes

52
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:37,456
through this left router and
then through these bottom routers, and

53
00:04:37,456 --> 00:04:40,228
we're receiving a stream of packets.

54
00:04:40,228 --> 00:04:43,074
So packet P1, P2, and P3.

55
00:04:43,074 --> 00:04:48,504
So P1 and
P2 get sent along this original path.

56
00:04:48,504 --> 00:04:53,712
But now let's say something happened in,
say, BGP, we got an update and

57
00:04:53,712 --> 00:04:58,028
we learned about a new path
where it's actually shorter.

58
00:04:58,028 --> 00:05:03,325
And so packet 3 might be
able to go that new path and

59
00:05:03,325 --> 00:05:10,666
get to this router on the right-hand
side before packets 1 and 2.

60
00:05:10,666 --> 00:05:16,128
And so that is a case of reordering
that would occur inside of the network,

61
00:05:16,128 --> 00:05:19,930
and it's just natural based
on how routing works.

62
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:26,108
So recall that TCP provides this

63
00:05:26,108 --> 00:05:31,476
abstraction of a reliable in order stream.

64
00:05:31,476 --> 00:05:35,288
So that's the service
model that TCP provides.

65
00:05:35,288 --> 00:05:40,111
So if we're transferring 300
bytes at the receiving side,

66
00:05:40,111 --> 00:05:43,296
we would expect to receive each of those,

67
00:05:43,296 --> 00:05:48,180
all 300 of those bytes,
and receive them in order.

68
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,900
So how do we do this?

69
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:55,800
So the first mechanism
that TCP has to allow for

70
00:05:55,800 --> 00:06:00,760
this to occur is the sequence number.

71
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:03,736
So we can see down here at the bottom,

72
00:06:03,736 --> 00:06:09,500
circled is a field within the TCP
header called Sequence number.

73
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,561
And so what the sequence number is,

74
00:06:12,561 --> 00:06:18,387
is it indicates which bytes
are contained in the datagram, right?

75
00:06:18,387 --> 00:06:22,699
So the actual number will
be the sequence number,

76
00:06:22,699 --> 00:06:27,934
the byte within the whole sequence,
which is, in our case,

77
00:06:27,934 --> 00:06:33,682
for 300 bytes, it would be,
which byte within that sequence of

78
00:06:33,682 --> 00:06:39,334
300 does this packet start with or
this datagram start with?

79
00:06:39,334 --> 00:06:44,854
And so shown there in each of these,
the length is 100.

80
00:06:44,854 --> 00:06:52,446
So this 0 represents bytes 0 to 99,
byte 100 to 199,

81
00:06:52,446 --> 00:06:57,658
200 to 299, and 300 to 399.

82
00:06:57,658 --> 00:07:02,421
And that would all occur because
the length is the same, but

83
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:05,000
they can be different lengths.

84
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:08,862
All right, so that's the sequence number.

85
00:07:08,862 --> 00:07:13,810
So what this allows is that
the receiver can now say which bytes

86
00:07:13,810 --> 00:07:17,680
in this sequence has it actually received.

87
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:20,720
So that's the first mechanism.

88
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:26,726
The second mechanism that TCP provides
is the acknowledgement number.

89
00:07:26,726 --> 00:07:31,484
So this is now another field
within the TCP header.

90
00:07:31,484 --> 00:07:36,264
And what it does is it
specifies to the sender, so

91
00:07:36,264 --> 00:07:42,676
it's the receiver sending back to
the sender what is the next byte

92
00:07:42,676 --> 00:07:48,760
in the stream that this
receiver expects to receive.

93
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:54,997
And so shown here is this is saying I
expect, I as the receiver of the stream,

94
00:07:54,997 --> 00:07:59,726
expect the next byte that I'm
going to get is byte 200.

95
00:07:59,726 --> 00:08:01,162
This is indicating or

96
00:08:01,162 --> 00:08:05,842
acknowledging that I've received
everything up until that point.

97
00:08:05,842 --> 00:08:10,525
So I've received 0 through 199.

98
00:08:13,313 --> 00:08:21,860
And then the third part of this mechanism
is the ability to do retransmissions.

99
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:27,940
So the sender can resend any
unacknowledged bytes in the stream.

100
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,181
So this is resending that
packet that contained

101
00:08:34,181 --> 00:08:38,736
bytes 200 to 299 in the sequence.

102
00:08:38,736 --> 00:08:44,611
And this all relates to if we go
back a couple slides, the receiver,

103
00:08:44,611 --> 00:08:49,973
this packet which is the sequence
number starting at 200,

104
00:08:49,973 --> 00:08:53,608
we're assuming that that was dropped.

105
00:08:53,608 --> 00:08:58,337
So the receiver received 0 sequence
number, a packet with 100 as

106
00:08:58,337 --> 00:09:03,602
the sequence number, and then a packet
as 300 as the sequence number.

107
00:09:03,602 --> 00:09:10,081
And so it acknowledged that, hey,
I received, or I'm acknowledging,

108
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:16,600
and the next byte that I expect is 200,
I haven't seen that yet.

109
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:22,390
And then the receiver or
the sender can resend 200.

110
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:27,648
So then the key question is,
when do you retransmit?

111
00:09:27,648 --> 00:09:32,446
Do you just retransmit
kind of right away or what?

112
00:09:32,446 --> 00:09:38,326
So there's two signals that we'll
cover that help the sender know when

113
00:09:38,326 --> 00:09:43,800
to retransmit, and it's a timeout and
those acknowledgments.

114
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:47,260
But it's a little more than
just resending immediately.

115
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:55,884
Now, taking it aside a little bit,
let's discuss what this diagram means.

116
00:09:55,884 --> 00:09:59,118
It's a message exchange diagram, and

117
00:09:59,118 --> 00:10:04,774
we'll see this when we kind of talk
about how these protocols work.

118
00:10:04,774 --> 00:10:10,362
So on the left, we have A,
and on the right, we have B.

119
00:10:10,362 --> 00:10:13,880
These are two parties
that are communicating.

120
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:19,342
And time is this kind of vertical line,
and it goes down,

121
00:10:19,342 --> 00:10:24,540
so it increases in time
as the line goes down.

122
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:28,026
So when you have a line like this,

123
00:10:28,026 --> 00:10:34,720
this would indicate a message
is sent by A and received by B.

124
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:39,344
And it's shown kind of on this diagonal
lower in the time to represent

125
00:10:39,344 --> 00:10:40,946
transmission time.

126
00:10:40,946 --> 00:10:43,781
So it takes some amount of time for

127
00:10:43,781 --> 00:10:48,050
the message to go through the network and
get to B.

128
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:54,453
When we see a message like
this with an X there,

129
00:10:54,453 --> 00:10:58,444
that represents a packet being dropped.

130
00:10:58,444 --> 00:11:03,107
And it doesn't necessarily
always have to be from B to A,

131
00:11:03,107 --> 00:11:07,850
we could have drawn that as one
of the messages from A to B.

132
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:13,848
But it's this kind of indication of it
that X represents a packet being dropped.

133
00:11:13,848 --> 00:11:19,000
If the lines crossed, that would
indicate a packet being reordered.

134
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:26,650
So now back to, oops, when to retransmit,

135
00:11:26,650 --> 00:11:32,640
the first thing to look at is the timeout.

136
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:40,758
So we send some frame or some datagram
with a sequence number of 500.

137
00:11:40,758 --> 00:11:42,900
Let's say it's length 10.

138
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:47,560
So this is expecting 500 to 509.

139
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:49,146
It gets received by B.

140
00:11:49,146 --> 00:11:53,556
And let's say B does send
an acknowledgment indicating that

141
00:11:53,556 --> 00:11:58,920
the next byte that it expects
to receive in the stream is 510.

142
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:03,020
But let's say that the packet
get dropped along the way.

143
00:12:03,020 --> 00:12:07,907
So how much time should the receiver or

144
00:12:07,907 --> 00:12:13,840
the sender A wait before it retransmits?

145
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:18,956
If you send it too soon,
let's say you sent it almost immediately

146
00:12:18,956 --> 00:12:23,978
after it sent the original message,
well, then there's going to

147
00:12:23,978 --> 00:12:30,100
be unnecessary messages,
datagrams going through the network.

148
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:34,932
So kind of the accepted
practice is to wait an amount

149
00:12:34,932 --> 00:12:38,842
of time that is based
on what you think and

150
00:12:38,842 --> 00:12:43,557
what the sender thinks
that the round trip time,

151
00:12:43,557 --> 00:12:48,158
the amount of transmission
time from A to B, and

152
00:12:48,158 --> 00:12:52,780
then back to A, and
then add in some buffer.

153
00:12:52,780 --> 00:12:57,304
And the way you can calculate
the estimated round trip time is through

154
00:12:57,304 --> 00:12:58,766
a formula like this.

155
00:12:58,766 --> 00:13:03,848
So each message, each datagram that
you're sending, you'll be able

156
00:13:03,848 --> 00:13:09,458
to know when you sent it and then when
you received an acknowledgment for it.

157
00:13:09,458 --> 00:13:13,680
And so
this is a sample of round trip time.

158
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:19,241
And then you can just take
this kind of estimated

159
00:13:19,241 --> 00:13:26,156
RTT over time by kind of adding
that to the overall factor.

160
00:13:26,156 --> 00:13:31,708
Now, this part, the 1 minus alpha and
the alpha help smooth that out.

161
00:13:31,708 --> 00:13:37,581
So you're not basically overreacting
to spikes in round trip time,

162
00:13:37,581 --> 00:13:43,454
and you're not underreacting to
when the round trip time changes.

163
00:13:43,454 --> 00:13:46,949
So you balance that out by
adding this alpha factor.

164
00:13:50,170 --> 00:13:56,208
The next signal of when to
retransmit is a duplicate ack.

165
00:13:56,208 --> 00:14:01,592
So just receiving an ack might
not necessarily indicate

166
00:14:01,592 --> 00:14:07,330
that a packet was dropped,
it might be out of order.

167
00:14:07,330 --> 00:14:11,434
So let's say the sequence is as follows.

168
00:14:11,434 --> 00:14:18,950
B previously acknowledged
sequence number starting at 500.

169
00:14:18,950 --> 00:14:22,683
So saying the next byte
that it expects is 500.

170
00:14:22,683 --> 00:14:29,016
A then sends 500, sends 510, and 520.

171
00:14:29,016 --> 00:14:35,374
So it's sending across multiple
packets the sequence from 500 to 530,

172
00:14:35,374 --> 00:14:39,624
if we're assuming all
the packets are length 10.

173
00:14:39,624 --> 00:14:42,950
And let's say this first one got dropped.

174
00:14:42,950 --> 00:14:45,909
Now, B did receive the others, and for

175
00:14:45,909 --> 00:14:49,767
each of those,
it's sending an acknowledgment,

176
00:14:49,767 --> 00:14:55,150
hey, I'm expecting the next
byte that I receive to be 500.

177
00:14:55,150 --> 00:14:58,022
And then when it receives this 520, hey,

178
00:14:58,022 --> 00:15:02,130
the next byte I'm expecting
to receive is 500.

179
00:15:02,130 --> 00:15:06,178
And so
this is a signal to the sender, to A,

180
00:15:06,178 --> 00:15:11,129
that when it receives
a duplicate acknowledgment,

181
00:15:11,129 --> 00:15:17,496
chances are the packet was dropped
rather than it was out of order.

182
00:15:17,496 --> 00:15:22,828
So you can imagine if 500
just happened to arrive

183
00:15:22,828 --> 00:15:27,540
after 510, B would have acknowledged and

184
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:31,804
said, hey, I'm looking for 500.

185
00:15:31,804 --> 00:15:36,830
A might have said, I've already sent that,
so let me resend it.

186
00:15:36,830 --> 00:15:39,686
But it was just out of order,
so it was unnecessary.

187
00:15:39,686 --> 00:15:44,459
But the second acknowledgment is
a stronger signal, not a guarantee, but

188
00:15:44,459 --> 00:15:48,110
a stronger signal that
the packet was actually dropped.

189
00:15:50,050 --> 00:15:54,311
So now last place that we
can talk about is really,

190
00:15:54,311 --> 00:15:57,434
what about drops at the receiver?

191
00:15:57,434 --> 00:16:03,830
So the sender may be able to send at
higher rates than the receiver can accept.

192
00:16:03,830 --> 00:16:10,930
So the goal here is to make it so that
the sender cannot overwhelm the receiver.

193
00:16:12,650 --> 00:16:17,132
This is where a concept
called flow control comes in.

194
00:16:17,132 --> 00:16:22,890
So how this is handled is
the receiver advertises the amount

195
00:16:22,890 --> 00:16:28,303
of free buffer space in a field
called the receive window

196
00:16:28,303 --> 00:16:34,350
in the TCP header, and
we'll see that in the next slide.

197
00:16:34,350 --> 00:16:40,206
And then the sender just limits
the amount of unacknowledged or

198
00:16:40,206 --> 00:16:47,330
in-flight data that's being sent to
whatever this receive window is.

199
00:16:47,330 --> 00:16:53,980
So this picture kind of illustrates
the buffer that at the receiver side,

200
00:16:53,980 --> 00:16:58,994
it has a certain amount of
data that's been received,

201
00:16:58,994 --> 00:17:07,080
it's acknowledged, it's waiting to deliver
it to the application, to the process.

202
00:17:08,330 --> 00:17:11,180
And so it has this amount of space left.

203
00:17:11,180 --> 00:17:13,896
And so it tells the sender, hey,

204
00:17:13,896 --> 00:17:18,170
here's how much space I
have in the receive window.

205
00:17:19,230 --> 00:17:24,140
So this field, you might see it
called just Window Size, but

206
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:29,051
it's this field in the TCP header,
and it indicates how many

207
00:17:29,051 --> 00:17:33,694
bytes the host is currently willing and
able to receive.

208
00:17:37,330 --> 00:17:41,831
So to summarize the kind of
different approaches, or

209
00:17:41,831 --> 00:17:47,559
all of the kind of aspects of TCP
that lead to the reliable transfer,

210
00:17:47,559 --> 00:17:51,000
we talked about the sequence number.

211
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:56,232
So that's a field inside
of the TCP header that

212
00:17:56,232 --> 00:18:01,868
indicates where in this
stream of bytes which byte

213
00:18:01,868 --> 00:18:07,046
number is this datagram actually covering.

214
00:18:07,046 --> 00:18:11,248
So the sequence number tells
what the first byte is.

215
00:18:11,248 --> 00:18:13,521
Acknowledgment number tells,

216
00:18:13,521 --> 00:18:18,610
these are the bytes that I've received,
and this is the next one.

217
00:18:18,610 --> 00:18:23,070
You set the acknowledgment number
to be the next byte that I'm

218
00:18:23,070 --> 00:18:24,801
expecting to receive.

219
00:18:24,801 --> 00:18:31,283
And then retransmission, so this is
the mechanism to be able to make sure that

220
00:18:31,283 --> 00:18:37,278
the receiver is receiving all of
the data that is part of that stream.

221
00:18:37,278 --> 00:18:42,859
And then the last thing that
we covered was flow control,

222
00:18:42,859 --> 00:18:49,704
which is just a receiver telling
the sender, my window size is this.

223
00:18:49,704 --> 00:18:52,040
In this picture, it says 500.

224
00:18:52,040 --> 00:18:54,448
And so then, this tells the sender,

225
00:18:54,448 --> 00:18:59,588
don't ever have more than 500 bytes
in-flight that are unacknowledged.

226
00:19:03,060 --> 00:19:08,172
All right, so next time we'll
talk a little more about TCP.

227
00:19:08,172 --> 00:19:08,819
All right, thanks.